# FigDown — figures as text. Spec: https://github.com/FigDown/figdown
# `CONNECTOR-IDENTITY-KEY`: the VERSION GATE on `id=`, REFUSING half. Case 813 is
# the accepting half and its directive lines are BYTE-IDENTICAL to this
# section's; the header is the only thing that differs. A gate is only a gate
# if both directions are pinned — this case alone would be satisfied by an
# engine that rejected `id=` everywhere, and 813 alone by one that accepted it
# everywhere. Keep them edited together.
#
# WHY THE KEY IS GATED, and the reason is NOT the one case 805 gives for
# `note=`. `note=` has a prior meaning on the record, so accepting it under an
# older header would repaint one figure as another. `id=` has no prior meaning
# of any kind: it was never a spelling of anything. It is gated because it is a
# NEW SPELLING ON THE ACCEPTED SURFACE, and a header that stops determining
# what the language accepts has stopped being a declaration. The frozen reading
# contract for a version, and the archived engine for it, both describe a
# language with no `id=`; an engine that accepted the key anyway would put a
# document outside every reader that holds it to the version it declares.
#
# ONE ERROR PER LINE. Each line below reports the gate and stops; the id-lexis
# check and the uniqueness check do not also fire.
figdown 0.4 block
node a "A"
node b "B"
node c "C"
edge a -> b id=e1
edge b -> c id=e2

# The version is interpolated TWICE in the message — once to report what the
# document declares and once to name what the spelling means there — so a
# second section at a different version pins both spellings the gate can
# produce. An engine that hard-coded "0.4" into the string fails here.
figdown 0.1 block
node p "P"
node q "Q"
edge p -- q id=e1
